  "fromId": "a_back",
  "toId": "schwa",
  "preamble": "Reduction of the open back vowel [ɑ] to schwa [ə] in unstressed syllables, with raising and centralization. Old English had back [ɑ] in unstressed syllables, as in dogga [doɡ.ɡɑ] 'dog' and nama /ˈnɑ.mɑ/ 'name'. In late Old English the unstressed vowels /ɑ e u/ fell together, and by Early Middle English all of them were written e, transcribed /ə/ in Middle English reconstructions. The final schwa was itself lost later.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Vowel Reduction, Centralization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Old English to Middle English",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old English nama /ˈnɑ.mɑ/ 'name'",
          "to": "Middle English name /ˈnaːm(ə)/",
          "note": "The stressed vowel was also lengthened in the open syllable; the final schwa was later lost."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English frocga (frogga) /ˈfroɡ.ɡɑ/ 'frog'",
          "to": "Middle English frogge /ˈfrɔɡ(ə)/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English drincan /ˈdrin.kɑn/ 'to drink'",
          "to": "Middle English drynken, drinken /ˈdrinkən/",
          "note": "Unstressed /ɑ/ before a consonant was reduced in the same way; the final /n/ of the infinitive was later dropped."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
